<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ragnachar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 509</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ragnachar was a Frankish king who ruled from Cambrai in the late 5th century, a relative of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis I</a>. He supported Clovis against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syagrius">Syagrius</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soissons">Soissons</a> in 486, but around 509 Clovis bribed his followers, defeated him and killed him and his brother Richar. The story comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a>, who portrays him as a debauched ruler who lost his warriors' loyalty.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ragnachar.jpg" alt="Ragnachar" width="320" height="465" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gustav Adolf Closs · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Frankish_prince_Ragnachar_is_killed_by_Clovis_after_losing_the_battle,_509_(G._Ad._Closs).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
